    88	    has a version of the same test: did the customer have a chance to see them before the deal, or
    89	    only after? On Dutch ground the box quotes the test and what fails it. A link in the footer of
    90	    the invoice, sent after the work was ordered, is the commonest way for a business to have terms
    91	    it cannot hold anyone to.</p>
    92	
    93	    <p><strong>The suppliers and platforms you depend on.</strong> Here the position is reversed.
    94	    You are the customer, and the terms you did not read are theirs. The payment processor's
    95	    reserve, the marketplace's right to delist, the software vendor's price change on thirty days'
    96	    notice, the landlord's break clause — every one is a term you agreed to by clicking, and every
    97	    one is written in the other side's favour, because they had a lawyer and you had a checkbox.
    98	    This chapter cannot tell you what they say. It can tell you that every one of them was drafted by a professional, and that the professional
    99	    was not yours.</p>
   100	
   101	    <p><strong>The world, about who pays.</strong> The fourth agreement is the one almost nobody
   102	    at this scale has: what happens when your work causes a loss to someone else. The customer's
   103	    site is down for a week because of your update; the food you supplied made someone ill; the
   104	    advice you gave was wrong and they acted on it. Somebody pays. Who — you, the business, an insurer — is a question with three parts: your legal
   105	    form, which is the fork <a href="12-reading-your-own-business.html">chapter twelve</a> met in
   106	    the filing rules and the notaris's subject rather than this book's; what your terms say and
   107	    whether they can be held to; and a policy you may or may not hold. This book gives no figure for what
   108	    such a loss could be, because any figure would be wrong for someone. It gives the shape: the
   109	    largest loss one customer could suffer from your mistake is a number, you can estimate it, and
   110	    the estimate is the question you take to an insurance adviser.</p>
   111	
   112	    <div class="ground">
   113	      <h3>On Dutch ground · who owns the work, and whether your terms bind</h3>
   114	
   115	      <p>If you are not in the Netherlands, skip to Move 3; the shape above holds, the defaults
   116	      differ. If you are, two of the four agreements have statutory answers this box can quote.
   117	      The other two — what your suppliers' terms say, and what you are liable for and insured
   118	      against — it cannot, and names the professional instead.</p>
   119	
   120	      <p><strong>Who owns the work.</strong> The Auteurswet starts from the maker: <em
   121	      lang="nl">"Het auteursrecht is het uitsluitend recht van den maker van een werk van
   122	      letterkunde, wetenschap of kunst, of van diens rechtverkrijgenden, om dit openbaar te maken
   123	      en te verveelvoudigen, behoudens de beperkingen, bij de wet gesteld."</em> Article 7 moves that default to the employer where making such works is what the employee is
   124	      employed to do, unless the contract says otherwise: <em lang="nl">"Indien de arbeid,
   125	      in dienst van een ander verricht, bestaat in het vervaardigen van bepaalde werken van
   126	      letterkunde, wetenschap of kunst, dan wordt, tenzij tusschen partijen anders is
   127	      overeengekomen, als de maker van die werken aangemerkt degene, in wiens dienst de werken
   128	      zijn vervaardigd."</em> Two conditions, then: in another's employ, and employed to make this kind of thing; and a
   129	      contract can undo the default. <em>In dienst van een ander</em> is the line, and a contractor
   130	      is not in your employ; <a href="19-the-first-hire.html">chapter nineteen</a>'s box is where
   131	      that line is drawn. For everyone who is not an employee, article
   132	      2 lid 3 governs: <em lang="nl">"De overeenkomst op grond waarvan het auteursrecht geheel of
   133	      gedeeltelijk wordt overgedragen of waarin een exclusieve licentie wordt verleend, wordt
   134	      schriftelijk aangegaan."</em> In writing. And when the person transferring is the one who made it — the freelancer, the
   135	      friend — the transfer reaches only as far as the writing goes: <em lang="nl">"De overdracht of
   136	      de verlening van een exclusieve licentie door de maker … omvat alleen die bevoegdheden die
   137	      uitdrukkelijk in de overeenkomst staan vermeld of die uit de aard en de strekking ervan
   138	      noodzakelijkerwijs voortvloeien."</em> Lid 5 takes that second sentence away where the
   139	      transferor is itself a maker under articles 7 or 8 — <em lang="nl">"Het derde lid, tweede
   140	      volzin, en het vierde lid zijn niet van toepassing op een maker als bedoeld in artikel 7 en
   141	      8"</em> — so an agency transferring its employees' work is the jurist's question, not this
   142	      box's.</p>
   143	
   144	      <p><strong>Whether your terms bind.</strong> Book 6 of the Civil Code, article 233: <em
   145	      lang="nl">"Een beding in algemene voorwaarden is vernietigbaar a. indien het, gelet op de
   184	    are the law's default plus the other side's memory, and they are read out on the day of the
   185	    dispute by whoever has the most to gain from their version.</strong> Writing the agreement down does not make the relationship a contract. It was one already; writing it down
   186	    makes it yours.</p>
   187	
   188	    <p>Ownership follows the writing, not the invoice — and this is the one that catches a business
   189	    that has done everything else right, because it looks paid for. The website, the brand, the
   190	    code: if they were made by someone you did not employ, the sentence that makes them yours
   191	    either exists or it does not, and the time to find out is before the person who made them has
   192	    moved on, raised their price, or started working for the competitor down the road. Terms bind
   193	    only if delivered: a limit on your liability that the customer never had the chance to read is
   194	    a limit on nothing. And the agreements you signed by clicking were drafted by a lawyer for the other side, which is
   195	    a reason to read them, not a reason to feel protected by them.</p>
   196	
   197	    <h3>When to pay someone who knows</h3>
   198	
   199	    <p>One failure is the handshake business that discovers its terms on
   200	    the day of the dispute. The other is the two-person studio with thirty pages of terms nobody
   201	    reads, copied from a company ten times its size, most of which would not survive being tested and all of which cost a customer's goodwill to present. <strong>A
   202	    professional's hour is owed when the agreement carries more than a month's revenue, lasts
   203	    longer than a year, contains the words <em>unlimited</em>, <em>indemnify</em> or
   204	    <em>exclusive</em>, or concerns something the business could not replace</strong> — and the
   205	    hour is cheap precisely because you arrive with the four agreements listed and the question
   206	    written down. Everything below that line is a template, read once, handed over in time, and
   207	    kept.</p>
   208	
   209	    <p>The boundary is that this chapter maps where the cost is; it does not say what any clause
   210	    should say, and it cannot, because the defaults differ by country and the right terms differ
   211	    by trade. A reader in the Netherlands has the box; a reader anywhere else has the same four
   212	    agreements and a different set of defaults, and the professional's hour is where the
   213	    difference is priced. What does not differ is the order: makers first, because the loss is
   214	    permanent; customers second, because it recurs; suppliers third, because you can only read,
   215	    not change; the world last, because it is the least likely and the largest.</p>
   216	  </section>
   217	
   218	  <section class="monday">
   219	    <h2>Monday</h2>
   220	    <ol>
   221	      <li><strong>List the four agreements as they exist today.</strong> Makers, customers, suppliers and platforms, liability. For each, three columns: written or not; where the writing is; who else has a copy. Any row that reads <em>no, nowhere, nobody</em> is a term somebody else will write.</li>
   222	      <li><strong>For every piece of work you rely on and did not make yourself, find the sentence that makes it yours.</strong> Site, logo, code, photographs, copy. If there is no sentence, ask the person who made it for one this week, while they still answer your emails — on Dutch ground, in writing, naming what is transferred, and on the lawyer's paper if the work is one the business could not replace.</li>
   223	      <li><strong>Move your terms from the invoice to the quote.</strong> Attach them to whatever the customer sees before they say yes, and keep the evidence that you did — the dated email, the signed page. Then read them once as the customer would, and cut anything you would refuse if it were put to you.</li>
   224	      <li><strong>Write the largest-loss sentence.</strong> <em>If we get this wrong, the customer loses …</em> — with a number, estimated, for the worst job you take. Take the sentence, the number and your legal form to an insurance adviser and ask what a policy against it costs. Decide with the two numbers side by side.</li>
   225	      <li><strong>Buy one hour.</strong> Take items 1 to 4 and the two agreements that carry the most money to a lawyer, and ask one question: <em>what have I agreed to that I do not know about?</em> Write the answer down and date it.</li>
   226	    </ol>
   227	  </section>
