   331	## 5b. On Dutch ground
   332	
   333	Where the answer depends on jurisdiction, the chapter carries a boxed `<div class="ground">` — the
   334	general argument stays portable, the local specifics stay quarantined, and neither rots the other.
   335	
   336	Rules, all of them non-negotiable because this is the part of the book most able to cause real harm:
   337	
   338	- **Never state Dutch law from memory.** Every claim in a ground box is sourced to a government or
   339	  official body — rijksoverheid.nl, KVK, Belastingdienst, UWV, wetten.overheid.nl, Rechtspraak.nl —
   340	  and added to `checks/claims.tsv` like any other figure.
   341	- **Date every box.** Rules move. Each box ends with a "checked" line carrying the date and the
   342	  source, so a reader in two years knows exactly how stale it is.
   343	- **Say who to ask.** The box's job is to tell the reader that a question exists and is expensive,
   344	  not to answer it definitively. Every box names the professional — accountant, employment lawyer,
   345	  notaris — whose job this actually is.
   346	- **No tax planning.** Structures, deductions and thresholds get named as things to ask about, never
   347	  recommended.
   348	
   349	Subjects known to need boxes, by chapter: side activities and non-compete clauses while still
   350	employed, and what resigning does to unemployment entitlement (05); VAT on invoices and whether you
   351	quote inclusive (09); payment terms and late payment (13); owner remuneration where the tax
   352	authority sets expectations for director-shareholders (15); the employee-versus-contractor
   353	distinction and its enforcement (19); ending an employment, notice, and severance (20); liability,
   354	professional indemnity insurance, standard terms, and who owns code written by an employee (24);
   355	how to wind up cleanly (28).
   356	
