  2527	task's time box reached.
  2528	
  2529	**Domain-competence declaration (§8).** 🇳🇱 chapter, carrying content a reader can act on and be
  2530	harmed by, so the standing rule applies: **never state Dutch law from memory.** Both statutes below
  2531	were fetched live during this task (not recalled, not paraphrased from a secondary source) and the
  2532	registered claims quote them verbatim. Two things this task explicitly did **not** do, because §6f
  2533	reserves them for whoever settles this chapter under its own process: it drew no conclusion about
  2534	what these articles mean for a specific reader's situation, and it did not compare the employee
  2535	default (Auteurswet art. 7) against the contractor gap (art. 2 lid 3) beyond stating what each article
  2536	says — that comparison is interpretive work belonging to the `.ground` box, not to a case-hunt entry.
  2537	One risk to flag now: art. 7's employer-default copyright applies only to work made "in dienst van een
  2538	ander" (in another's employ) — an ordinary employment relationship — and does **not** extend to
  2539	independent contractors or freelancers, who keep authorship, and whose copyright can only be
  2540	transferred by a **written** instrument under art. 2 lid 3. A chapter (or a reader) that assumes
  2541	"we paid for it, so we own it" for contractor-built code would be relying on a default that does not
  2542	exist. This is precisely the kind of scope error §6f's ch. 15 review caught twice for other chapters
  2543	and it is the single most important sentence this entry can hand forward.
