  2545	**Registered — statutory claims, no company case.** `checks/sources.tsv` gains one new source,
  2546	`auteurswet-2026` (wetten.overheid.nl/BWBR0001886/2026-01-01, a dated consolidation that — like
  2547	`lb-12a` and unlike Books 2 and 6 of the BW — serves full article bodies at a dated URL, fetched live
  2548	during this task, 2026-08-06). `bw6-2026` already exists (registered for the distance-contract
  2549	withdrawal right) and is reused here for the algemene-voorwaarden articles, per §6b — same pattern as
  2550	`bw7-2026`'s reuse across ch. 05/19/20. Four claims added to `checks/claims.tsv` under chapter 24: the
  2551	Auteurswet art. 7 employer-default sentence, the Auteurswet art. 2 lid 3 written-transfer sentence, the
  2552	BW6 art. 233 voidability grounds (the full a./b. clause), and the BW6 art. 234 terhandstelling clause.
  2553	`./verify.sh --strict 24` passes all four (4 verified, 0 drifted, 0 skipped), confirmed by a live
  2554	fetch run during this task — `auteurswet-2026` fetched fresh (200, 1,076,473 bytes) and `bw6-2026`
  2555	served from cache.
  2556	
  2557	### Ch. 25 — What Protects You
  2558	
  2559	**Status: has a case.**
  2560	
  3270	**Write claims against the filing's own words, not against the prose.** The claim rows are the
  3271	sentences the filing actually contains; the chapter then paraphrases them. This way a rewrite of the
  3272	prose cannot silently break the sourcing, and a claim that fails tells you the filing changed or the
  3273	citation was wrong — never that a sentence got reworded.
  3274	
  3275	### Two kinds of accuracy, and the script only checks one
  3276	
  3277	Added after external review found chapter 4 asserting that Galperin *"did exactly what this chapter
  3278	asks"* while its own next-but-one paragraph said he *"chose a consequence he had not written down in
  3279	advance"* — a direct contradiction, in a block where **every claim row passed and still does**.
  3280	
  3281	| | What it means | What checks it |
  3282	|---|---|---|
  3283	| **Factual** | the numbers, dates and quotations are present in the cited source | `verify.sh`, mechanically |
  3284	| **Interpretive** | the case actually supports the lesson the chapter draws from it | nothing but reading |
  3285	
  3286	The second is where this genre fails, and it is invisible to the tooling by construction. A chapter
  3287	can be assembled entirely from correctly quoted figures and still be wrong about what they show.
  3288	`verify.sh` makes the first kind of error nearly impossible and does nothing whatever about the
  3289	second, which means the tooling's existence is itself a hazard: it produces a feeling of rigour that
  3290	covers half the surface. Say so publicly rather than letting the script imply more than it does.
  3291	
  3292	Two rules follow.
  3293	
  3294	**Score the case against the chapter's own test, clause by clause.** Where a chapter states a
  3295	numbered test — ch. 04's three components, ch. 02's five extractions — write the case against every
  3296	numbered item before claiming it fits. Ch. 04's counter-case met one and a half of three, and nobody
  3297	noticed because every sentence in it was true.
  3298	
  3299	**The error is recursive, and that is the thing to internalise.** Chapter 4's Gymdesk block has now
  3300	been corrected three times, each pass catching a finer-grained version of the same move:
  3301	
  3302	| Pass | The overclaim | What was actually true |
  3303	|---|---|---|
  3304	| 1 | "counter-case: the rule, honoured, would have been wrong" | he never followed the rule |
  3305	| 2 | "did exactly what this chapter asks" | one and a half of three components |
  3306	| 3 | "the pair make the same point: the third component is load-bearing" | the case supports *widening the menu of consequences*; it does not establish **choosing one in advance**, because Galperin improvised and succeeded |
  3307	| 4 | "it says nothing about choosing in advance — if anything it cuts the other way" | **the correction overshot.** It does not show pre-selection was *necessary*, but it does show the mechanism pre-selection guards against, running in real time: with no consequence written down he took the decision under pressure, at the worst moment, as the person least able to take it. Good judgement, not a plan. |
  3308	
  3309	Each fix was honest and each left a smaller instance of itself behind, because the pull is not toward
  3310	one wrong sentence but toward extracting the maximum lesson a case will bear. Pass 4 shows the pull
  3311	also runs backwards: **"the case is silent on this" is itself a claim about the case, and needs the
  3312	same test as "the case proves this."** Over-correction reads as rigour and costs the chapter a real
  3313	piece of evidence.
  3314	
  3315	The countermeasure is mechanical, not moral: **after fixing an overclaim, re-read the corrected
  3355	warehouses Webvan never built, it was the first one it did.
  3356	
  3357	Keep this table. It is the standing argument for why the book is built this way.
  3358	
  3359	### Ch. 19–28 drafted in one day — what the two review instruments found
  3360	
  3361	Written 2026-08-28. Ten chapters drafted and craft-reviewed (one Fable reviewer per chapter, reports
  3362	in `checks/craft/`), with `scripts/review.sh` run on ch. 19 only (grok and agy independent, codex
  3363	consolidating; report in `checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/`). What recurred, in order of frequency:
  3364	
  3365	1. **The chapter reading the source for what it ought to show.** Every first draft added words the
  3366	   source did not contain — "for years", "by the hour", "as a freelancer", "fully remote",
  3367	   "deliberately", "ahead of need", "end to end", "close to half" — and in four chapters reordered
  3368	   the source's sentences into a causal sequence (19's agencies, 21's standup and video, 23's hire
  3369	   and Happy %, 26's features and June). The reviewers caught all of it by reading the raw source
  3370	   beside the chapter; nothing else would have.
  3371	2. **Dutch law leaking out of the box** in looser English: ch. 19's Monday turned a presumption into
  3372	   a determination; ch. 20 sent a performance dismissal to the UWV (art. 671a is grounds a and b;
  3373	   ground d is the kantonrechter under 671b); ch. 24 glossed art. 2 lid 3's scope clause onto
  3374	   transfers by non-makers (lid 5 excludes art. 7/8 makers) and stated the terms mechanism backwards
  3375	   ("bind nobody" — they bind and are voidable). All corrected with claim rows from the registered
  3376	   statutes. Rule confirmed: the portable paragraph must stay portable; the box quotes.
  3377	3. **Instruments borrowed from the wrong chapter**: ch. 22 forgot ch. 12's weekly three and ch. 21's
  3378	   reader; ch. 26 borrowed ch. 23's hours-reserve for a demand shock; ch. 28's Monday pointed at
  3379	   ch. 23 for a months figure that lives in ch. 13 and ch. 16. The fix each time was one reconciling
  3380	   sentence and a corrected pointer.
  3381	4. **Caveats said three or four times** (Move 2, Move 3, owed, Reading) where §6d allows one in the
  3382	   argument. Cut to once in every chapter.
  3383	5. **Counting openers and self-announcing climaxes** in every draft; cut.
  3384	
  3385	The codex consolidation on ch. 19 agreed with the craft reviewer on the presumption overshoot and
  3386	the agency causation, and added three the craft pass had not: "therefore" in the decision box
  3387	(task/job does not determine legal form), the funding test without a decision date (a licence to
  3388	wait — §3's own rule), and an unregistered figure carried from the ch. 18 source. All applied.
  3389	review.sh was not run on 20–28 (grok's quota is unreliable; the craft reviewer caught the same class
  3390	of error) — running it on the four 🇳🇱 chapters (20, 24, 28 and 19 again) before the user's read is
    90	gloss) but to make Move 2 portable, as it says it is, and let the two signposts do the pointing.
    91	This is a zero-fact fix and the one that most changes what the chapter claims.
    92	
    93	**Rewrite** — makers: *"Whether you own it is a separate question, and the answer is not in the
    94	invoice. Everywhere this book knows of, the default starts with the person who made the work, and
    95	the routes by which it becomes the business's are few, specific, and written; which routes, and
    96	what the writing has to say, are jurisdiction's — the box quotes the Dutch ones. What is portable is
    97	the negative: paying for the work is not one of the routes. Paying the invoice transfers nothing."*
    98	— customers: *"They protect you only to the extent the customer can be held to them, and every
    99	jurisdiction has a version of the same test: did the customer have a chance to see them before the
   100	deal, or only after? On Dutch ground the box quotes the test and what fails it. A link in the footer
   101	of the invoice, sent after the work was ordered, is the commonest way for a business to have terms
   102	it cannot hold anyone to."*
   103	
   104	## 2. The box applies art. 2 lid 3's scope clause to "everyone who is not an employee"; the statute confines it to a transfer by the maker, and lid 5 switches it off for arts. 7 and 8
   105	
   106	**Quote** — Box: *"For everyone who is not an employee, article 2 lid 3 governs: "De overeenkomst
   107	op grond waarvan het auteursrecht geheel of gedeeltelijk wordt overgedragen of waarin een
   108	exclusieve licentie wordt verleend, wordt schriftelijk aangegaan." In writing — and only as far as
   109	the writing goes: the transfer "omvat alleen die bevoegdheden die uitdrukkelijk in de overeenkomst
   110	staan vermeld of die uit de aard en de strekking ervan noodzakelijkerwijs voortvloeien.""*
   111	
   112	**Lens** — Dutch ground (§5b: nothing overstated) / the §6i risk.
   113	
   114	**What's wrong** — The first sentence of lid 3 (writing) is general and the gloss is right. The
   115	second sentence is not general. In the consulted text it reads: *"De overdracht of de verlening van
   116	een exclusieve licentie door de maker of door een natuurlijke persoon die het auteursrecht als
   117	erfgenaam of legataris van de maker heeft verkregen, omvat alleen die bevoegdheden die
   118	uitdrukkelijk in de overeenkomst staan vermeld…"* — the chapter's quotation starts after "door de
   119	maker of door een natuurlijke persoon…", which is the clause that limits it. And lid 5: *"Het derde
   120	lid, tweede volzin, en het vierde lid zijn niet van toepassing op een maker als bedoeld in artikel
   121	7 en 8."* So the "only as far as the writing goes" rule protects a natural-person maker — the
   122	freelancer, the friend — and does not apply where the transferor is an employer-maker (art. 7) or
   123	a legal person deemed maker (art. 8), which is what "the agency" in Move 2 usually is. As written,
   124	the box tells a reader that a transfer from an agency-bv is confined to what the writing expressly
   125	says; the statute says the opposite for that transferor. Also worth noting, not for the box: the
   126	same lid's third sentence requires *"een daartoe bestemde akte"* for delivery, which is stronger
   127	than "in writing" and is another reason the invoice is not the instrument.
   128	
   129	**Rewrite** — `pending fact-check` (lid 3 second sentence in full, and lid 5, need claim rows;
   130	same registered source): *"For everyone who is not an employee, article 2 lid 3 governs: "De
   131	overeenkomst op grond waarvan het auteursrecht geheel of gedeeltelijk wordt overgedragen of waarin
   132	een exclusieve licentie wordt verleend, wordt schriftelijk aangegaan." In writing. And when the
   133	person transferring is the one who made it — the freelancer, the friend — the transfer reaches only
   134	as far as the writing goes: "De overdracht of de verlening van een exclusieve licentie door de
   135	maker … omvat alleen die bevoegdheden die uitdrukkelijk in de overeenkomst staan vermeld of die uit
   136	de aard en de strekking ervan noodzakelijkerwijs voortvloeien." Lid 5 takes that second sentence
   137	away where the transferor is itself a maker under articles 7 or 8 — an agency transferring its
   138	employees' work is the jurist's question, not this box's."* Zero-fact fallback: keep the quotation
   139	as is and change the gloss to *"In writing — and, for a transfer by the person who made it, only
   140	as far as the writing goes:"*, and in Move 2 (finding 1's rewrite already does this) drop "the
   141	agency" from the list of makers to whom the scope rule is said to apply.
   142	
   143	## 3. "Work made in employment" is wider than art. 7's condition, and the article's own exception is not glossed
   144	
   145	**Quote** — Box: *"Article 7 moves that default to the employer for work made in employment:"* —
   146	Move 2: *"Work made by an employee, in the course of employment, is by default the employer's."*
   147	
   148	**Lens** — Dutch ground (§5b) / faithful gloss.
   149	
   150	**What's wrong** — Art. 7 as quoted: *"Indien de arbeid, in dienst van een ander verricht, bestaat
   300	
   301	**Lens** — Seams (mode 5) / §5b leak / the practitioner's ear.
   302	
   303	**What's wrong** — The chapter's own first sentence has the reader signing "a lease, a bank
   304	mandate", both of which were drafted by someone's lawyer, so "the only agreements … drafted by a
   305	professional" is false on the chapter's own terms; and the sentence is made twice, once per Move.
   306	The observation that survives is the good one — those terms were drafted for the other side. "Set
   307	aside as onerous" is art. 233 onder a's language ("onredelijk bezwarend") applied, as a prediction
   308	with a fraction attached ("half"), to a template of any jurisdiction; it is the box's test used as
   309	a universal outside it. The paragraph's point — that borrowed big-company terms cost goodwill and
   310	would not survive testing — does not need the fraction or the statutory verb.
   311	
   312	**Rewrite** — Move 2: *"It can tell you that every one of them was drafted by a professional, and
   313	that the professional was not yours."* — Move 3: *"And the agreements you signed by clicking were
   314	drafted by a lawyer for the other side, which is a reason to read them, not a reason to feel
   315	protected by them."* — Move 3: *"most of which would not survive being tested and all of which cost
   316	a customer's goodwill to present"*.
   317	
   318	## 9. Monday 2's Dutch clause and Monday 4's "legal form" are the right amount of law; one word in Monday 2 overreaches
   319	
   320	**Quote** — Monday 2: *"If there is no sentence, ask the person who made it for one this week,
   321	while they still answer your emails — on Dutch ground, in writing, and naming what is transferred."*
   322	
   323	**Lens** — §5b / Monday as failable action.
   324	
   325	**What's wrong** — This is the acceptable form: signposted "on Dutch ground", and it restates only
   326	what the box quotes (schriftelijk; uitdrukkelijk vermeld). It stays. One caution follows from
   327	finding 2: "naming what is transferred" is the right instruction for a freelancer's transfer, and
   328	harmless for an agency's; keep it. What overreaches is "ask the person who made it for one" — a
   329	sentence in an email is not the *akte* lid 3's third sentence requires for delivery, and the box
   330	does not quote that sentence. Since the chapter cannot state it without a claim row, the Monday
   331	should not imply that an emailed sentence closes the question.
   332	
   333	**Rewrite** — *"If there is no sentence, ask the person who made it for one this week, while they
   334	still answer your emails — on Dutch ground, in writing, naming what is transferred, and on the
   335	lawyer's paper if the work is one the business could not replace."* (The last clause is the
   336	chapter's own threshold from Move 3, not a legal claim.)
